 ((15
  ("Self-adjusting Computation with Delta ML" 0.1445 0.8547 0.0770 0.0964 0.0964 0.0194 0.0128 0.3421 0.2230)
  ("Umut A. Acar^{1} and Ruy Ley-Wild^{2}" 0.3178 0.6800 0.1318 0.1477 0.1477 0.0136 0.0078 0.3561 0.0579)
  ("1" 0.3465 0.3550 0.1618 0.1736 0.1736 0.0118 nil nil 0.0085)
  ("Toyota Technological Institute" 0.3669 0.6523 0.1668 0.1790 0.1790 0.0122 0.0077 0.2304 0.0643)
  ("Chicago, IL, USA" 0.4163 0.5827 0.1834 0.1956 0.1956 0.0122 0.0074 0.3235 0.0804)
  ("umut@tti-c.org" 0.4227 0.5751 0.2012 0.2126 0.2126 0.0114 nil 0.0000 0.1524)
  ("2" 0.3610 0.3695 0.2116 0.2234 0.2234 0.0118 nil nil 0.0085)
  ("Carnegie Mellon University" 0.3814 0.6377 0.2166 0.2288 0.2288 0.0122 0.0076 0.3435 0.0818)
  ("Pittsburgh, PA, USA" 0.3995 0.5995 0.2332 0.2454 0.2454 0.0122 0.0072 0.3226 0.1065)
  ("rleywild@cs.cmu.edu" 0.3953 0.6022 0.2510 0.2624 0.2624 0.0114 nil 0.0000 0.2068)
  ("Abstract. In self-adjusting computation, programs respond automati-" 0.1632 0.8353 0.3070 0.3226 0.3226 0.0122 0.0083 0.2787 0.1009)
  ("cally and eﬃciently to modiﬁcations to their data by tracking the" 0.1632 0.8356 0.3270 0.3392 0.3392 0.0122 0.0127 0.2462 0.0431)
  ("dynamic data dependences of the computation and incrementally updat-" 0.1632 0.8358 0.3437 0.3559 0.3559 0.0122 0.0060 0.2819 0.0793)
  ("ing the output as needed. In this tutorial, we describe the self-adjusting-" 0.1632 0.8350 0.3603 0.3725 0.3725 0.0122 0.0064 0.2590 0.0286)
  ("computation model and present the language ΔML (Delta ML) for" 0.1632 0.8354 0.3733 0.3910 0.3890 0.0122 0.0123 0.2709 0.1177)
  ("writing self-adjusting programs." 0.1631 0.4609 0.3935 0.4057 0.4057 0.0122 0.0074 0.2953 0.0665)
  ("1" 0.0973 0.1129 0.4409 0.4571 0.4571 0.0162 nil nil 0.0157)
  ("Introduction" 0.1442 0.3165 0.4409 0.4571 0.4571 0.0162 nil 0.1898 0.1724)
  ("Since the early years of computer science, researchers realized that many uses of" 0.0973 0.9017 0.4789 0.4924 0.4924 0.0136 0.0067 0.2589 0.0528)
  ("computer applications are incremental by nature. We start an application with" 0.0973 0.9014 0.4970 0.5106 0.5106 0.0136 0.0079 0.2878 0.0954)
  ("some initial input to obtain some initial output. We then observe the output," 0.0973 0.9016 0.5150 0.5286 0.5286 0.0136 0.0095 0.2729 0.0504)
  ("make some small modiﬁcations to the input and re-compute the output. We" 0.0973 0.9015 0.5332 0.5468 0.5468 0.0136 0.0106 0.2783 0.0530)
  ("often repeat this process of modifying the input incrementally and re-computing" 0.0973 0.9020 0.5514 0.5650 0.5650 0.0136 0.0066 0.2956 0.0509)
  ("the output. In many applications, incremental modiﬁcations to the input cause" 0.0973 0.9019 0.5694 0.5830 0.5830 0.0136 0.0081 0.2724 0.0321)
  ("only incremental modiﬁcations to the output, raising the question of whether it" 0.0973 0.9016 0.5876 0.6011 0.6011 0.0136 0.0074 0.2743 0.0432)
  ("would be possible to update the output faster than recomputing from scratch." 0.0973 0.8909 0.6056 0.6191 0.6191 0.0136 0.0077 0.2519 0.0601)
  ("Examples of this phenomena abound. For example, applications that interact" 0.1235 0.9019 0.6238 0.6373 0.6373 0.0136 0.0069 0.2896 0.0979)
  ("with or model the physical world (e.g., robots, traﬃc control systems, schedul-" 0.0973 0.9016 0.6420 0.6555 0.6555 0.0136 0.0081 0.2875 0.0450)
  ("ing systems) observe the world evolve slowly over time and must respond to" 0.0973 0.9015 0.6600 0.6735 0.6735 0.0136 0.0095 0.2595 0.0309)
  ("those changes eﬃciently. Similarly in applications that interact with the user," 0.0973 0.9017 0.6781 0.6917 0.6917 0.0136 0.0095 0.2568 0.0531)
  ("application-data changes incrementally over time as a result of user commands." 0.0973 0.9015 0.6963 0.7099 0.7099 0.0136 0.0074 0.2857 0.1652)
  ("For example, in software development, the compiler is invoked repeatedly after" 0.0973 0.9015 0.7143 0.7279 0.7279 0.0136 0.0081 0.2763 0.0339)
  ("the user makes small changes to the program code. Other example applica-" 0.0973 0.9019 0.7325 0.7461 0.7461 0.0136 0.0114 0.2468 0.0321)
  ("tion areas include databases, scientiﬁc computing (e.g., physical simulations)," 0.0973 0.9016 0.7507 0.7642 0.7642 0.0136 0.0100 0.2525 0.0400)
  ("graphics, etc." 0.0973 0.2313 0.7687 0.7822 0.7822 0.0136 0.0072 0.2139 0.0907)
  ("In many of the aforementioned examples, modiﬁcations to the computation" 0.1235 0.9017 0.7869 0.8004 0.8004 0.0136 0.0091 0.2753 0.0212)
  ("data or input are external (e.g., the user modiﬁes some data). In others, incre-" 0.0973 0.9019 0.8049 0.8184 0.8184 0.0136 0.0082 0.2477 0.0452)
  ("mental modiﬁcations are inherent. For example, in motion simulation, objects" 0.0973 0.9017 0.8230 0.8366 0.8366 0.0136 0.0092 0.2972 0.0689)
  ("move continuously over time causing the property being computed to change" 0.0973 0.9019 0.8412 0.8548 0.8548 0.0136 0.0100 0.2233 0.0525)
  ("continuously as well. In particular, if we wish to simulate the ﬂow of a ﬂuid by" 0.0973 0.9015 0.8592 0.8728 0.8728 0.0136 0.0081 0.2851 0.1271)
  ("modeling its constituent particles, then we need to compute certain properties" 0.0973 0.9018 0.8774 0.8910 0.8910 0.0136 0.0087 0.2457 0.0920)
  ("of moving objects, e.g., we may want to triangulate the particles to compute the" 0.0973 0.9012 0.8956 0.9091 0.9091 0.0136 0.0066 0.3095 0.0188)
  ("P. Koopman and D. Swierstra (Eds.): AFP 2008, LNCS 5832, pp. 1–38, 2009." 0.0973 0.7263 0.9318 0.9413 0.9413 0.0095 0.0062 0.2930 0.0164)
  ("c" 0.1023 0.1106 0.9436 0.9531 0.9531 0.0095 nil nil 0.0082)
  (" Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2009" 0.0973 0.4385 0.9413 0.9550 0.9535 0.0095 0.0060 0.3191 0.0185))
  (16
  ("2" 0.0973 0.1080 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107)
  ("U.A. Acar and R. Ley-Wild" 0.1740 0.4348 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 0.0072 0.4138 0.0442)
  ("forces exerted between particles, and update those properties as the points move." 0.0973 0.9019 0.0816 0.0951 0.0951 0.0136 0.0058 0.2699 0.0576)
  ("Since the combinatorial structure of the computed properties change slowly over" 0.0973 0.9021 0.0998 0.1133 0.1133 0.0136 0.0064 0.2735 0.0527)
  ("time, we can often view continuous motion as an incremental modiﬁcation; this" 0.0973 0.9016 0.1179 0.1315 0.1315 0.0136 0.0075 0.3075 0.0514)
  ("makes it possible to compute the output more eﬃciently than re-computing it" 0.0973 0.9015 0.1359 0.1495 0.1495 0.0136 0.0089 0.2782 0.0622)
  ("from-scratch at ﬁxed intervals." 0.0973 0.4077 0.1541 0.1677 0.1677 0.0136 0.0077 0.2536 0.1270)
  ("Although incremental applications abound, no eﬀective general-purpose tech-" 0.1235 0.9016 0.1723 0.1859 0.1859 0.0136 0.0067 0.2590 0.0948)
  ("nique or language was known for developing incremental applications until" 0.0973 0.9015 0.1903 0.2039 0.2039 0.0136 0.0128 0.2325 0.0547)
  ("recently (see Section 10 for the discussion of the earlier work on the subject)." 0.0973 0.9014 0.2085 0.2220 0.2220 0.0136 0.0092 0.1954 0.0801)
  ("Many problems required designing speciﬁc techniques or data structures for re-" 0.0973 0.9018 0.2265 0.2400 0.2400 0.0136 0.0077 0.2522 0.0575)
  ("membering and re-using results to ensure that computed properties may be" 0.0973 0.9013 0.2447 0.2582 0.2582 0.0136 0.0114 0.2352 0.1120)
  ("updated eﬃciently under incremental modiﬁcations to data. Recent advances" 0.0973 0.9017 0.2629 0.2764 0.2764 0.0136 0.0105 0.2389 0.0830)
  ("on self-adjusting computation (Section 10.2) oﬀer an alternative by proposing" 0.0973 0.9019 0.2809 0.2944 0.2944 0.0136 0.0094 0.2201 0.0246)
  ("general-purpose techniques for automatically adapting computations to data" 0.0973 0.9016 0.2990 0.3126 0.3126 0.0136 0.0116 0.2247 0.1598)
  ("modiﬁcations by selectively re-executing the parts of the computation that de-" 0.0973 0.9015 0.3172 0.3308 0.3308 0.0136 0.0083 0.2381 0.1347)
  ("pend on the modiﬁcations and re-using unaﬀected parts. Applications of the" 0.0973 0.9015 0.3352 0.3488 0.3488 0.0136 0.0107 0.2194 0.0495)
  ("technique to problems from a reasonably diverse set of areas show that the ap-" 0.0973 0.9016 0.3534 0.3670 0.3670 0.0136 0.0079 0.2461 0.0966)
  ("proach can be eﬀective both in theory and practice." 0.0973 0.6223 0.3716 0.3851 0.3851 0.0136 0.0078 0.2026 0.0680)
  ("We present a tutorial on a language for self-adjusting computation, called" 0.1235 0.9018 0.3896 0.4031 0.4031 0.0136 0.0106 0.2517 0.0321)
  ("ΔML (Delta ML), that extends the Standard ML (SML) language with primi-" 0.0973 0.9016 0.4078 0.4213 0.4213 0.0136 0.0087 0.3219 0.0522)
  ("tives for self-adjusting computation." 0.0973 0.4627 0.4258 0.4393 0.4393 0.0136 0.0077 0.2705 0.0466)
  ("In self-adjusting computation, programs consist of two components: a self-" 0.1235 0.9016 0.4440 0.4575 0.4575 0.0136 0.0102 0.2621 0.0212)
  ("adjusting core and a top- or meta-level mutator. The self-adjusting core is a" 0.0973 0.9015 0.4621 0.4757 0.4757 0.0136 0.0106 0.2417 0.0934)
  ("purely functional program that performs a single run of the intended applica-" 0.0973 0.9020 0.4801 0.4937 0.4937 0.0136 0.0092 0.2419 0.0640)
  ("tion. The mutator drives the self-adjusting core by supplying the initial input" 0.0973 0.9015 0.4983 0.5119 0.5119 0.0136 0.0092 0.2466 0.0464)
  ("and by subsequently modifying data based on the application. The mutator can" 0.0973 0.9019 0.5165 0.5301 0.5301 0.0136 0.0070 0.2670 0.0374)
  ("modify the computation data in a variety of forms depending on the application." 0.0973 0.9018 0.5345 0.5481 0.5481 0.0136 0.0064 0.3042 0.0701)
  ("For example, in a physical simulation, the mutator can insert a new object into" 0.0973 0.9015 0.5527 0.5662 0.5662 0.0136 0.0075 0.2917 0.0339)
  ("the set of objects being considered. In motion simulation, the mutator changes" 0.0973 0.9019 0.5707 0.5842 0.5842 0.0136 0.0082 0.2633 0.0321)
  ("the outcomes of comparisons performed between objects as the relationship be-" 0.0973 0.9015 0.5889 0.6024 0.6024 0.0136 0.0075 0.2688 0.0322)
  ("tween objects change because of motion. After modifying computation data," 0.0973 0.9018 0.6070 0.6206 0.6206 0.0136 0.0109 0.2497 0.0580)
  ("the mutator can update the output and the computation by requesting change" 0.0973 0.9013 0.6250 0.6386 0.6386 0.0136 0.0083 0.2133 0.0321)
  ("propagation to be performed. Change propagation is at the core of self-adjusting" 0.0973 0.9016 0.6432 0.6568 0.6568 0.0136 0.0067 0.2633 0.1163)
  ("computation: it an automatic mechanism for propagating the data modiﬁcations" 0.0973 0.9016 0.6614 0.6750 0.6750 0.0136 0.0062 0.3150 0.1340)
  ("through the computation to update the output." 0.0973 0.5809 0.6794 0.6930 0.6930 0.0136 0.0078 0.2196 0.0802)
  ("To support eﬃcient change propagation, we represent a computation with a" 0.1235 0.9016 0.6976 0.7111 0.7111 0.0136 0.0084 0.2444 0.0264)
  ("trace that records the data and control dependences in the computation. Change" 0.0973 0.9022 0.7156 0.7291 0.7291 0.0136 0.0063 0.2573 0.0483)
  ("propagation uses the trace to identify and re-execute the parts of the compu-" 0.0973 0.9017 0.7338 0.7473 0.7473 0.0136 0.0095 0.1932 0.1216)
  ("tation that depend on the modiﬁed data while re-using the parts unaﬀected" 0.0973 0.9015 0.7520 0.7655 0.7655 0.0136 0.0108 0.2010 0.0607)
  ("by the changes. The structure and the representation of the trace is critical to" 0.0973 0.9014 0.7700 0.7835 0.7835 0.0136 0.0084 0.2000 0.0245)
  ("the eﬀectiveness of the change propagation. Techniques have been developed for" 0.0973 0.9015 0.7881 0.8017 0.8017 0.0136 0.0070 0.2224 0.0321)
  ("implementing both tracing and change propagation eﬃciently (Section 10.2)." 0.0973 0.8769 0.8063 0.8199 0.8199 0.0136 0.0078 0.2666 0.1370)
  ("The ΔML language provides linguistic facilities for writing self-adjusting pro-" 0.1235 0.9020 0.8243 0.8379 0.8379 0.0136 0.0070 0.3117 0.0399)
  ("grams consisting of a core and a mutator. To this end, the language distinguishes" 0.0973 0.9016 0.8425 0.8561 0.8561 0.0136 0.0060 0.2979 0.0610)
  ("between two kinds of function spaces: conventional and self-adjusting. The mu-" 0.0973 0.9016 0.8605 0.8741 0.8741 0.0136 0.0078 0.2649 0.0817)
  ("tator consists solely of conventional functions. The self-adjusting core consists" 0.0973 0.9018 0.8787 0.8922 0.8922 0.0136 0.0092 0.2171 0.0504))
  (17
  ("Self-adjusting Computation with Delta ML" 0.4191 0.8246 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 0.0073 0.3379 0.1273)
  ("3" 0.8907 0.9014 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107)
  ("of self-adjusting functions and all pure (self-adjusting or conventional) functions" 0.0973 0.9018 0.0816 0.0951 0.0951 0.0136 0.0067 0.2522 0.0188)
  ("that they call directly or indirectly (transitively)." 0.0973 0.5973 0.0998 0.1133 0.1133 0.0136 0.0077 0.2286 0.0426)
  ("ΔML enables the programmer to mark the computation data that is expected" 0.1235 0.9016 0.1179 0.1315 0.1315 0.0136 0.0066 0.3164 0.0522)
  ("to change across runs (or over time) by placing them into modiﬁable references" 0.0973 0.9014 0.1359 0.1495 0.1495 0.0136 0.0082 0.2521 0.0205)
  ("or modiﬁables for short. For implementing a self-adjusting core, ΔML provides" 0.0973 0.9018 0.1541 0.1677 0.1677 0.0136 0.0088 0.3003 0.0208)
  ("facilities for creating and reading modiﬁables within a self-adjusting function. In" 0.0973 0.9018 0.1723 0.1859 0.1859 0.0136 0.0066 0.2926 0.0831)
  ("this tutorial, we do not include the update operation on modiﬁables in the core—" 0.0973 0.9021 0.1903 0.2039 0.2039 0.0136 0.0058 0.3001 0.0374)
  ("modiﬁables are write-once within the self-adjusting core.^{1} ΔML also provides" 0.0973 0.9018 0.2062 0.2220 0.2220 0.0136 0.0101 0.2776 0.1141)
  ("facilities for deﬁning self-adjusting functions to be memoized if so desired. For" 0.0973 0.9016 0.2265 0.2400 0.2400 0.0136 0.0088 0.2672 0.0832)
  ("implementing a mutator, ΔML provides meta-level facilities to create, read, and" 0.0973 0.9016 0.2447 0.2582 0.2582 0.0136 0.0070 0.3389 0.1369)
  ("update modiﬁables, and to perform change propagation. The mutator can use the" 0.0973 0.9014 0.2629 0.2764 0.2764 0.0136 0.0053 0.3162 0.0700)
  ("update operation to modify destructively the contents of modiﬁables—this is how" 0.0973 0.9016 0.2809 0.2944 0.2944 0.0136 0.0053 0.3343 0.0701)
  ("mutators modify the inputs of the self-adjusting core. After such modiﬁcations" 0.0973 0.9015 0.2990 0.3126 0.3126 0.0136 0.0084 0.2781 0.0913)
  ("are performed, the mutator can use change propagation to update the result of" 0.0973 0.9018 0.3172 0.3308 0.3308 0.0136 0.0078 0.2312 0.0312)
  ("the core." 0.0973 0.1853 0.3352 0.3488 0.3488 0.0136 0.0078 0.1448 0.0322)
  ("Writing a self-adjusting program is very similar to writing a conventional," 0.1235 0.9023 0.3534 0.3670 0.3670 0.0136 0.0107 0.2844 0.0773)
  ("purely functional program. Using the techniques described in this tutorial, it" 0.0973 0.9016 0.3716 0.3851 0.3851 0.0136 0.0103 0.2457 0.0639)
  ("is not hard to take an existing purely functional SML program and make it" 0.0973 0.9015 0.3896 0.4031 0.4031 0.0136 0.0106 0.2434 0.0156)
  ("self-adjusting by annotating the code with ΔML primitives. Annotated code is" 0.0973 0.9015 0.4078 0.4213 0.4213 0.0136 0.0085 0.2861 0.1340)
  ("guaranteed to respond to modiﬁcations to its data correctly: the result of an" 0.0973 0.9018 0.4258 0.4393 0.4393 0.0136 0.0101 0.1983 0.1119)
  ("updated run is equivalent to a (from-scratch) run. Guaranteeing eﬃcient change" 0.0973 0.9019 0.4440 0.4575 0.4575 0.0136 0.0067 0.2740 0.0830)
  ("propagation, however, may require some additional eﬀort: we sometimes need to" 0.0973 0.9015 0.4621 0.4757 0.4757 0.0136 0.0065 0.3111 0.1280)
  ("modify the algorithm or use a diﬀerent algorithm to achieve the optimal update" 0.0973 0.9014 0.4801 0.4937 0.4937 0.0136 0.0070 0.3030 0.0700)
  ("times." 0.0973 0.1578 0.4983 0.5119 0.5119 0.0136 nil 0.4073 0.0606)
  ("When an algorithm does not yield to eﬃcient change propagation, it is some-" 0.1235 0.9017 0.5165 0.5301 0.5301 0.0136 0.0071 0.3197 0.0598)
  ("times possible to change it slightly to regain eﬃciency, often by eliminating" 0.0973 0.9014 0.5345 0.5481 0.5481 0.0136 0.0113 0.2670 0.0541)
  ("unnecessary dependences between computation data and control. For example," 0.0973 0.9018 0.5527 0.5662 0.5662 0.0136 0.0082 0.2254 0.1210)
  ("the eﬀectiveness of a self-adjusting mergesort algorithm can be improved by em-" 0.0973 0.9015 0.5707 0.5842 0.5842 0.0136 0.0066 0.3010 0.0322)
  ("ploying a divide-and-conquer strategy that divides the input into two sublists" 0.0973 0.9016 0.5889 0.6024 0.6024 0.0136 0.0094 0.2158 0.0736)
  ("randomly instead of deterministically dividing in the middle. Using randomiza-" 0.0973 0.9023 0.6070 0.6206 0.6206 0.0136 0.0078 0.3165 0.0963)
  ("tion eliminates the dependence between the length of the list and the compu-" 0.0973 0.9017 0.6250 0.6386 0.6386 0.0136 0.0092 0.2381 0.0399)
  ("tation, making the computation less sensitive to modiciations to the input (e.g." 0.0973 0.9016 0.6432 0.6568 0.6568 0.0136 0.0076 0.2825 0.0671)
  ("when a new element is inserted the input length changes, causing the divide-and-" 0.0973 0.9019 0.6614 0.6750 0.6750 0.0136 0.0060 0.2946 0.0528)
  ("conquer algorithm to create diﬀerent splits than before the insertion, ultimately" 0.0973 0.9014 0.6794 0.6930 0.6930 0.0136 0.0070 0.2726 0.0793)
  ("preventing re-use). Sometimes, such small changes to the algorithm do not suﬃce" 0.0973 0.9015 0.6976 0.7111 0.7111 0.0136 0.0056 0.3361 0.1065)
  ("to improve its eﬃciency and we need to consider an entirely diﬀerent algorithm." 0.0973 0.9019 0.7156 0.7291 0.7291 0.0136 0.0070 0.2878 0.0206)
  ("For example, the quicksort algorithm is inherently more sensitive to input mod-" 0.0973 0.9015 0.7338 0.7473 0.7473 0.0136 0.0070 0.3032 0.0339)
  ("iﬁcations than the mergesort algorithm, because it is sensitive to values of the" 0.0973 0.9013 0.7520 0.7655 0.7655 0.0136 0.0087 0.2447 0.0904)
  ("pivots, whereas the mergesort algorithm is not. Similarly, an algorithm that" 0.0973 0.9019 0.7700 0.7835 0.7835 0.0136 0.0111 0.2819 0.0673)
  ("sums a list of numbers by performing a traversal of the list and maintaining" 0.0973 0.9017 0.7881 0.8017 0.8017 0.0136 0.0102 0.2691 0.0504)
  ("an accumulator will not yield to eﬃcient change propagation, because inserting" 0.0973 0.9014 0.8063 0.8199 0.8199 0.0136 0.0076 0.2667 0.0245)
  ("an element can change the value of the accumulator at every recursive call. No" 0.0973 0.9015 0.8243 0.8379 0.8379 0.0136 0.0080 0.2478 0.0246)
  ("1" 0.1037 0.1122 0.8749 0.8867 0.8867 0.0118 nil nil 0.0085)
  ("The actual ΔML language places no such restrictions on how many time modiﬁables" 0.1205 0.9013 0.8764 0.8941 0.8921 0.0122 0.0063 0.3193 0.0369)
  ("can be written in the core." 0.1205 0.3697 0.8966 0.9088 0.9088 0.0122 0.0073 0.2381 0.0320))
  (18
  ("4" 0.0973 0.1080 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107)
  ("U.A. Acar and R. Ley-Wild" 0.1740 0.4348 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 0.0072 0.4138 0.0442)
  ("small modiﬁcation will improve this algorithm as we would like. Considering a" 0.0973 0.9016 0.0816 0.0951 0.0951 0.0136 0.0084 0.3267 0.0530)
  ("diﬀerent, random sampling algorithm addresses the problem (Section 8)." 0.0973 0.8329 0.0998 0.1133 0.1133 0.0136 0.0077 0.2884 0.0902)
  ("The structure of the rest of the tutorial is as follows. In Section 2 we de-" 0.1235 0.9015 0.1179 0.1315 0.1315 0.0136 0.0104 0.2145 0.0398)
  ("scribe how incremental modiﬁcations arise and why they can lead to improved" 0.0973 0.9018 0.1359 0.1495 0.1495 0.0136 0.0084 0.2689 0.0587)
  ("eﬃciency and why having general-purpose techniques and languages can help" 0.0973 0.9016 0.1541 0.1677 0.1677 0.0136 0.0098 0.2025 0.0922)
  ("take advantage of this potential. In Section 3 we describe the self-adjusting com-" 0.0973 0.9016 0.1723 0.1859 0.1859 0.0136 0.0064 0.2729 0.0427)
  ("putation model and the core and the meta primitives for writing self-adjusting" 0.0973 0.9016 0.1903 0.2039 0.2039 0.0136 0.0083 0.2811 0.0864)
  ("programs. In Section 4 we describe an example self-adjusting application, called" 0.0973 0.9017 0.2085 0.2220 0.2220 0.0136 0.0070 0.2860 0.1012)
  ("CIRCLES, and how the user can interact with such a program. In the rest of the" 0.0973 0.9014 0.2265 0.2400 0.2400 0.0136 0.0072 0.2752 0.0969)
  ("tutorial, we use this example to illustrate how the ΔML language may be used" 0.0973 0.9016 0.2447 0.2582 0.2582 0.0136 0.0081 0.2972 0.0828)
  ("to implement self-adjusting programs." 0.0973 0.4826 0.2629 0.2764 0.2764 0.0136 0.0077 0.3184 0.0205)
  ("2" 0.0973 0.1129 0.3064 0.3226 0.3226 0.0162 nil nil 0.0157)
  ("Motivation" 0.1442 0.2942 0.3064 0.3226 0.3226 0.0162 nil 0.3970 0.1500)
  ("We consider the two kinds of modiﬁcations, discrete and continuous, that arise in" 0.0973 0.9017 0.3434 0.3570 0.3570 0.0136 0.0058 0.3187 0.0321)
  ("incremental applications via simple examples and describe how we may take ad-" 0.0973 0.9014 0.3616 0.3751 0.3751 0.0136 0.0067 0.3137 0.1181)
  ("vantage of them to improve eﬃciency. We then describe how and why language-" 0.0973 0.9021 0.3798 0.3933 0.3933 0.0136 0.0070 0.3025 0.0775)
  ("based techniques are critical for scalability." 0.0973 0.5333 0.3978 0.4113 0.4113 0.0136 0.0076 0.2280 0.0569)
  ("2.1" 0.0973 0.1315 0.4418 0.4552 0.4552 0.0135 nil 0.2779 0.0342)
  ("Discrete and Continuous Modifications" 0.1578 0.6131 0.4418 0.4552 0.4552 0.0135 0.0090 0.3091 0.0961)
  ("Close inspection of incremental applications reveal that two kinds of modiﬁcation" 0.0973 0.9016 0.4707 0.4842 0.4842 0.0136 0.0053 0.3234 0.0543)
  ("arise naturally: discrete/dynamic and continuous/kinetic." 0.0973 0.6795 0.4889 0.5024 0.5024 0.0136 0.0075 0.2486 0.0468)
  ("Discrete/Dynamic: A discrete or dynamic modiﬁcation is a combinatorial" 0.0973 0.9011 0.5201 0.5337 0.5336 0.0136 0.0116 0.2960 0.2203)
  ("modiﬁcation to the input of the program that modiﬁes the set of objects" 0.1366 0.9015 0.5381 0.5517 0.5517 0.0136 0.0100 0.2399 0.1256)
  ("in the input." 0.1366 0.2640 0.5563 0.5699 0.5699 0.0136 0.0078 0.2531 0.0193)
  ("Continuous/Kinetic: A continuous or kinetic modiﬁcation aﬀects the rela-" 0.0973 0.9018 0.5741 0.5877 0.5876 0.0136 0.0107 0.2615 0.2379)
  ("tionship between the input objects but does not aﬀect the set of objects" 0.1366 0.9016 0.5921 0.6057 0.6057 0.0136 0.0105 0.1869 0.0812)
  ("itself. By a relationship we broadly mean any function mapping objects into" 0.1366 0.9017 0.6103 0.6239 0.6239 0.0136 0.0069 0.3004 0.0548)
  ("a discrete set. For example, comparisons between objects are relationships" 0.1366 0.9018 0.6285 0.6421 0.6421 0.0136 0.0090 0.2472 0.0116)
  ("because the co-domain contains true and false." 0.1366 0.6345 0.6465 0.6604 0.6601 0.0136 0.0076 0.2196 0.0782)
  ("As an example, consider sorting a list of numbers and how discrete and contin-" 0.0973 0.9017 0.6781 0.6917 0.6917 0.0136 0.0077 0.2708 0.0264)
  ("uous modiﬁcations may arise in this application." 0.0973 0.5891 0.6963 0.7099 0.7099 0.0136 0.0076 0.2912 0.0466)
  ("Suppose that we sort a list of numbers, e.g., [30,10,20,0], and then we" 0.1235 0.9016 0.7143 0.7282 0.7279 0.0136 0.0109 0.2142 0.0832)
  ("insert a new number to the list, e.g. [7,30,10,20,0]. Since the set of objects" 0.0973 0.9017 0.7325 0.7464 0.7460 0.0136 0.0087 0.2361 0.0570)
  ("itself is modiﬁed this is a discrete modiﬁcation. Note that inserting/deleting" 0.0973 0.9016 0.7507 0.7642 0.7642 0.0136 0.0110 0.2643 0.0483)
  ("an element from the input only causes an in incremental modiﬁcation to the" 0.0973 0.9014 0.7687 0.7822 0.7822 0.0136 0.0094 0.2753 0.0246)
  ("output, that of inserting/deleting the new element into/from the output (at the" 0.0973 0.9014 0.7869 0.8004 0.8004 0.0136 0.0072 0.2714 0.0747)
  ("right position). Thus we can expect to be able to update the output signiﬁcantly" 0.0973 0.9019 0.8049 0.8184 0.8184 0.0136 0.0064 0.2653 0.0487)
  ("faster than recomputing from scratch. In fact, we can update the output for a" 0.0973 0.9016 0.8230 0.8366 0.8366 0.0136 0.0086 0.2381 0.0563)
  ("sorting application in optimal logarithmic time, instead of the O(n log n) time a" 0.0973 0.9014 0.8412 0.8548 0.8548 0.0136 0.0069 0.3462 0.0700)
  ("re-computation would require." 0.0973 0.4041 0.8592 0.8728 0.8728 0.0136 0.0075 0.2710 0.1549)
  ("As an example of continuous modiﬁcations, consider a set of numbers that" 0.1235 0.9018 0.8774 0.8910 0.8910 0.0136 0.0096 0.2487 0.0264)
  ("change as a function of time a(t) = 40.0 − 0.3t, b(t) = 20.0 − 0.3t, c(t) = 10, and" 0.0973 0.9016 0.8917 0.9113 0.9091 0.0136 0.0066 0.3592 0.0692))
  (19
  ("Self-adjusting Computation with Delta ML" 0.4191 0.8246 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 0.0073 0.3379 0.1273)
  ("output change" 0.4949 0.5981 0.0956 0.1054 0.1054 0.0098 0.0045 0.2463 0.0453)
  ("a(t) = 40.0 - 0.3 t" 0.4773 0.5981 0.1062 0.1160 0.1160 0.0098 0.0045 0.3319 0.0244)
  ("b(t) = 20.0 - 0.3 t" 0.4773 0.5981 0.1168 0.1267 0.1267 0.0098 0.0045 0.3319 0.0244)
  ("c(t) = 10.0" 0.5244 0.5981 0.1274 0.1373 0.1373 0.0098 0.0045 0.3193 0.0235)
  ("d(t) = 0.15 t" 0.5144 0.5981 0.1381 0.1479 0.1479 0.0098 0.0045 0.3296 0.0244)
  ("40" 0.3319 0.3500 0.1274 0.1372 0.1372 0.0098 nil nil 0.0181)
  ("Positions" 0.2964 0.3115 0.1414 0.1844 0.1844 0.0059 nil nil 0.0151)
  ("5" 0.8907 0.9014 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107)
  ("20" 0.3319 0.3500 0.1692 0.1790 0.1790 0.0098 nil nil 0.0181)
  ("10" 0.3319 0.3500 0.1901 0.1999 0.1999 0.0098 nil nil 0.0181)
  ("0" 0.3409 0.3500 0.2110 0.2208 0.2208 0.0098 nil nil 0.0091)
  ("0" 0.3575 0.3665 0.2425 0.2523 0.2523 0.0098 nil nil 0.0091)
  ("50" 0.4324 0.4505 0.2425 0.2523 0.2523 0.0098 nil nil 0.0181)
  ("100" 0.5073 0.5344 0.2425 0.2523 0.2523 0.0098 nil 0.0000 0.0272)
  ("Time" 0.5005 0.5367 0.2584 0.2682 0.2682 0.0098 nil 0.4547 0.0362)
  ("150" 0.5867 0.6139 0.2425 0.2523 0.2523 0.0098 nil 0.0001 0.0272)
  ("200" 0.6661 0.6933 0.2425 0.2523 0.2523 0.0098 nil 0.0000 0.0272)
  ("Fig. 1. Numbers varying with time" 0.3345 0.6644 0.2797 0.2954 0.2953 0.0156 0.0069 0.3578 0.0418)
  ("d = 0.15t. Suppose that we want to keep the numbers sorted as they change over" 0.0973 0.9022 0.3279 0.3415 0.3415 0.0136 0.0063 0.2764 0.0120)
  ("time. More speciﬁcally, we want to start at time zero (t = 0), and then update" 0.0973 0.9013 0.3459 0.3595 0.3595 0.0136 0.0081 0.2982 0.0514)
  ("the output whenever the ordering changes. Since time changes continuously," 0.0973 0.9019 0.3641 0.3777 0.3777 0.0136 0.0114 0.1994 0.0321)
  ("the values of the functions also change continuously. But the sorting changes" 0.0973 0.9019 0.3823 0.3959 0.3959 0.0136 0.0100 0.1946 0.0321)
  ("discretely—only when the outcomes of comparisons between the moving numbers" 0.0973 0.9016 0.4003 0.4139 0.4139 0.0136 0.0051 0.3447 0.1628)
  ("change." 0.0973 0.1730 0.4185 0.4320 0.4320 0.0136 nil 0.0998 0.0757)
  ("For example, at time 0, sorted output is [d(t), c(t), b(t), a(t)], which remains" 0.1235 0.9018 0.4365 0.4501 0.4500 0.0136 0.0086 0.3123 0.0339)
  ("the same until the time becomes 33.3̇, when b(t) falls below c(t) changing the out-" 0.0973 0.9015 0.4547 0.4682 0.4682 0.0136 0.0056 0.3467 0.0321)
  ("put to [d(t), b(t), c(t), a(t)]. The output then remains the same until 44.4̇, when" 0.0973 0.9016 0.4729 0.4864 0.4864 0.0136 0.0080 0.3128 0.0347)
  ("b(t) falls below d(t), and the output becomes [b(t), d(t), c(t), a(t)]. The output" 0.0973 0.9014 0.4909 0.5044 0.5044 0.0136 0.0094 0.2761 0.0364)
  ("then changes at 66.6̇ to [b(t), c(t), d(t), a(t)], and at 88.8̇ to [b(t), c(t), a(t), d(t)]." 0.0973 0.9010 0.5090 0.5226 0.5226 0.0136 0.0077 0.2708 0.0450)
  ("The ﬁnal change takes place at time 100.0, when the output becomes" 0.0973 0.9016 0.5272 0.5408 0.5408 0.0136 0.0169 0.2626 0.0399)
  ("[b(t), a(t), c(t), d(t)]. Although the output changes continuously, we only need to" 0.0973 0.9016 0.5452 0.5588 0.5588 0.0136 0.0069 0.2812 0.0491)
  ("update the computation at these times, i.e., when the output changes" 0.0973 0.9019 0.5634 0.5770 0.5770 0.0136 0.0176 0.2906 0.0701)
  ("combinatorially." 0.0973 0.2595 0.5814 0.5950 0.5950 0.0136 nil 0.3089 0.1622)
  ("Note that when the outcome of the comparison changes, the change in the" 0.1235 0.9013 0.5996 0.6131 0.6131 0.0136 0.0095 0.2250 0.0483)
  ("output is small—it is simply a swap of two adjacent numbers. This property" 0.0973 0.9018 0.6178 0.6313 0.6313 0.0136 0.0101 0.3004 0.0683)
  ("enables treating motion as a form of incremental modiﬁcation. In fact, in this" 0.0973 0.9016 0.6358 0.6493 0.6493 0.0136 0.0091 0.2845 0.0735)
  ("example, we can model continuous modiﬁcations as discrete modiﬁcations that" 0.0973 0.9016 0.6540 0.6675 0.6675 0.0136 0.0081 0.2787 0.0895)
  ("aﬀect the outcomes of comparisons. In general, if the computed property only" 0.0973 0.9016 0.6721 0.6857 0.6857 0.0136 0.0090 0.2497 0.0547)
  ("depends on relationships between data whose values range over a discrete do-" 0.0973 0.9015 0.6901 0.7037 0.7037 0.0136 0.0092 0.1925 0.0817)
  ("main, then we can perform motion simulation by changing the values of these" 0.0973 0.9016 0.7083 0.7219 0.7219 0.0136 0.0089 0.2743 0.0567)
  ("relationships as they take diﬀerent values discretely." 0.0973 0.6248 0.7265 0.7401 0.7401 0.0136 0.0076 0.2116 0.1281)
  ("2.2" 0.0973 0.1314 0.7732 0.7867 0.7867 0.0135 nil 0.2847 0.0342)
  ("Taking Advantage of Incrementality" 0.1577 0.5781 0.7732 0.7867 0.7867 0.0135 0.0093 0.2643 0.0786)
  ("When an incremental modiﬁcation to computation data causes an incremental" 0.0973 0.9017 0.8049 0.8184 0.8184 0.0136 0.0086 0.2853 0.0598)
  ("modiﬁcation to the output, we can expect to update the output faster than by" 0.0973 0.9016 0.8230 0.8366 0.8366 0.0136 0.0082 0.2197 0.1256)
  ("re-computing from scratch. In this tutorial, we propose language-based general" 0.0973 0.9020 0.8412 0.8548 0.8548 0.0136 0.0081 0.2506 0.1342)
  ("purpose techniques for taking advantage of incremental modiﬁcations to update" 0.0973 0.9014 0.8592 0.8728 0.8728 0.0136 0.0070 0.2551 0.0795)
  ("outputs faster. An alternative approach would be to design and implement ad" 0.0973 0.9018 0.8774 0.8910 0.8910 0.0136 0.0089 0.2502 0.0772)
  ("hoc data structures, called dynamic data structures, on a per-problem basis. In" 0.0973 0.9017 0.8956 0.9091 0.9091 0.0136 0.0080 0.2474 0.0354))
  (20
  ("6" 0.0973 0.1080 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107)
  ("U.A. Acar and R. Ley-Wild" 0.1740 0.4348 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 0.0072 0.4138 0.0442)
  ("this section, we brieﬂy overview the design of several dynamic data structures" 0.0973 0.9018 0.0816 0.0951 0.0951 0.0136 0.0090 0.2314 0.0374)
  ("for some relatively simple problems and point out some diﬃculties with the" 0.0973 0.9013 0.0998 0.1133 0.1133 0.0136 0.0111 0.2896 0.0279)
  ("approach." 0.0973 0.1964 0.1179 0.1315 0.1315 0.0136 nil 0.1133 0.0991)
  ("As a simple example, consider mapping a list to another list. Here is the" 0.1235 0.9013 0.1359 0.1495 0.1495 0.0136 0.0110 0.2764 0.0265)
  ("interface for a DynamicMap data structure:" 0.0973 0.5265 0.1541 0.1680 0.1677 0.0136 0.0076 0.1932 0.0865)
  ("signature DynamicMap =" 0.0973 0.3648 0.1788 0.1915 0.1915 0.0126 0.0113 0.0276 0.1104)
  ("sig" 0.0973 0.1340 0.1968 0.2095 0.2095 0.0126 nil 0.0000 0.0367)
  ("type (α,β) t" 0.1338 0.2847 0.2150 0.2277 0.2277 0.0127 0.0121 0.0725 0.0490)
  ("val map: α list -> (α -> β) -> β list * (α,β) t" 0.1338 0.7201 0.2318 0.2457 0.2453 0.0126 0.0121 0.0628 0.0367)
  ("val insert: α * int * (α,β) t -> β list * (α,β) t" 0.1338 0.7446 0.2500 0.2639 0.2635 0.0126 0.0121 0.0618 0.0367)
  ("end" 0.0973 0.1340 0.2694 0.2820 0.2820 0.0126 nil 0.0000 0.0367)
  ("The map function performs an “initial map” of the input and generates the" 0.0973 0.9015 0.2925 0.3064 0.3060 0.0136 0.0113 0.2283 0.0399)
  ("output list as well as a data structure (of abstract type (α,β) t) that can be" 0.0973 0.9013 0.3105 0.3244 0.3241 0.0136 0.0086 0.2040 0.0683)
  ("used to speedup the subsequent insertions. After map is executed, we can modify" 0.0973 0.9012 0.3287 0.3426 0.3422 0.0136 0.0065 0.2744 0.0452)
  ("the input by inserting new elements using the insert operation. This operation" 0.0973 0.9020 0.3467 0.3606 0.3602 0.0136 0.0070 0.2693 0.0322)
  ("takes the new element with the position at which is should be inserted and the" 0.0973 0.9013 0.3649 0.3784 0.3784 0.0136 0.0078 0.2633 0.0519)
  ("data structure, and returns the updated output list and data structure." 0.0973 0.8225 0.3830 0.3966 0.3966 0.0136 0.0077 0.2005 0.0451)
  ("Having designed the interface, let’s consider two possible choices for the aux-" 0.1235 0.9016 0.4010 0.4146 0.4146 0.0136 0.0073 0.2496 0.0716)
  ("iliary data structure that is used to speed up the insertions." 0.0973 0.7049 0.4192 0.4328 0.4328 0.0136 0.0077 0.2159 0.0525)
  ("1. The auxiliary data structure is the input, the map function simply returns the" 0.1071 0.9014 0.4438 0.4577 0.4573 0.0136 0.0057 0.3006 0.0182)
  ("input. The insert function inserts the element into the list at the speciﬁed" 0.1366 0.9017 0.4620 0.4759 0.4755 0.0136 0.0077 0.2571 0.0603)
  ("position. To update the output we have no choice but to re-execute, which" 0.1366 0.9016 0.4800 0.4935 0.4935 0.0136 0.0084 0.2219 0.0871)
  ("oﬀers no performance gain." 0.1366 0.4121 0.4981 0.5117 0.5117 0.0136 0.0076 0.2476 0.0538)
  ("2. The auxiliary data structure represents the input and the output along with" 0.1071 0.9014 0.5150 0.5286 0.5286 0.0136 0.0067 0.2505 0.0182)
  ("pointers linking the input elements to the corresponding output elements." 0.1366 0.9017 0.5332 0.5468 0.5468 0.0136 0.0096 0.2348 0.0814)
  ("These pointers help in ﬁnding the location of the element corresponding to" 0.1366 0.9015 0.5514 0.5650 0.5650 0.0136 0.0081 0.2441 0.0593)
  ("an input element in the output. The map function constructs and returns" 0.1366 0.9018 0.5694 0.5833 0.5830 0.0136 0.0100 0.2109 0.0246)
  ("this data structure. The insert function inserts the element in the input" 0.1366 0.9014 0.5876 0.6015 0.6011 0.0136 0.0097 0.2232 0.0374)
  ("at the speciﬁed position, maps the element to an output element, and using" 0.1366 0.9014 0.6058 0.6193 0.6193 0.0136 0.0074 0.2827 0.0207)
  ("the pointers to the output, ﬁnds the position for the output element, and" 0.1366 0.9018 0.6238 0.6373 0.6373 0.0136 0.0095 0.2128 0.0321)
  ("inserts it. Using this approach, we can insert a new element and update" 0.1366 0.9013 0.6420 0.6555 0.6555 0.0136 0.0107 0.2349 0.0660)
  ("the output with expected constant time overhead over the time it takes to" 0.1366 0.9015 0.6600 0.6735 0.6735 0.0136 0.0086 0.2327 0.0322)
  ("map the input element to an output. Note that we can update the input in" 0.1366 0.9018 0.6781 0.6917 0.6917 0.0136 0.0078 0.2647 0.0439)
  ("(expected) constant time by maintaining a mapping between locations and" 0.1366 0.9017 0.6963 0.7099 0.7099 0.0136 0.0082 0.2616 0.1069)
  ("the pointers to update." 0.1366 0.3703 0.7143 0.7279 0.7279 0.0136 0.0077 0.1996 0.0321)
  ("As our second example, consider a dynamic data structure for sorting with the" 0.0973 0.9014 0.7401 0.7537 0.7537 0.0136 0.0080 0.2611 0.0265)
  ("following interface." 0.0973 0.2879 0.7581 0.7717 0.7717 0.0136 0.0074 0.2840 0.0904)
  ("signature DynamicSort =" 0.0973 0.3771 0.7881 0.8008 0.8008 0.0126 0.0113 0.0271 0.1104)
  ("sig" 0.0973 0.1340 0.8061 0.8188 0.8188 0.0126 nil 0.0000 0.0367)
  ("type α t" 0.1339 0.2339 0.8230 0.8369 0.8366 0.0127 0.0122 0.0747 0.0490)
  ("val sort: α list -> (α * α -> bool) -> α list * α t" 0.1339 0.7678 0.8412 0.8551 0.8548 0.0126 0.0121 0.0655 0.0367)
  ("val insert: (α * int) -> α t -> α list * α t" 0.1339 0.6799 0.8592 0.8731 0.8728 0.0126 0.0121 0.0634 0.0367)
  ("end" 0.0973 0.1340 0.8787 0.8913 0.8913 0.0127 nil 0.0000 0.0367))
  (21
  ("Self-adjusting Computation with Delta ML" 0.4191 0.8246 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 0.0073 0.3379 0.1273)
  ("7" 0.8907 0.9014 0.0435 0.0557 0.0557 0.0122 nil nil 0.0107)
  ("The sort operation performs an “initial sort” of the input and generates the" 0.0973 0.9015 0.0816 0.0951 0.0951 0.0136 0.0103 0.2127 0.0399)
  ("output list as well as the auxiliary data structure (of abstract type α t). After" 0.0973 0.9012 0.0998 0.1137 0.1133 0.0136 0.0080 0.2392 0.0683)
  ("sort is executed, we can change the input by inserting new elements using the" 0.0973 0.9012 0.1179 0.1319 0.1315 0.0136 0.0081 0.2420 0.0490)
  ("insert operation. This operation takes the new element with the position at" 0.0973 0.9019 0.1359 0.1499 0.1495 0.0136 0.0098 0.2331 0.0736)
  ("which it should be inserted and the data structure, and returns the updated" 0.0973 0.9015 0.1541 0.1677 0.1677 0.0136 0.0103 0.1865 0.0586)
  ("output list and data structure." 0.0973 0.4079 0.1723 0.1859 0.1859 0.0136 0.0077 0.2075 0.0683)
  ("As before, there are choices for the auxiliary data structure." 0.1235 0.7327 0.1903 0.2039 0.2039 0.0136 0.0077 0.2246 0.0265)
  ("1. The sort function returns the input as the auxiliary data structure. The" 0.1071 0.9017 0.2212 0.2351 0.2348 0.0136 0.0101 0.2080 0.0182)
  ("insert operation simply modiﬁes the input and sorts from scratch to update" 0.1366 0.9013 0.2392 0.2531 0.2528 0.0136 0.0058 0.3054 0.0736)
  ("the output. This is equivalent to a from-scratch execution." 0.1366 0.7294 0.2574 0.2710 0.2710 0.0136 0.0077 0.2550 0.0321)
  ("2. The sort function returns both input and the output as the auxiliary data" 0.1071 0.9017 0.2758 0.2897 0.2893 0.0136 0.0079 0.2235 0.0182)
  ("structure. The insert operation inserts the new element into the input and" 0.1366 0.9017 0.2938 0.3077 0.3073 0.0136 0.0072 0.2590 0.0982)
  ("into the output. In this case, the insert operation can be performed in O(n)" 0.1366 0.9014 0.3119 0.3259 0.3255 0.0136 0.0062 0.2929 0.0392)
  ("time." 0.1366 0.1880 0.3301 0.3437 0.3437 0.0136 nil 0.4313 0.0514)
  ("3. The sort function builds a balanced binary-tree representation of the input" 0.1071 0.9015 0.3483 0.3622 0.3619 0.0136 0.0072 0.2421 0.0182)
  ("list and returns it as the auxiliary data structure. The nodes of the balanced" 0.1366 0.9020 0.3665 0.3800 0.3800 0.0136 0.0066 0.2555 0.0310)
  ("binary search tree point to their place in the output list. The insert op-" 0.1366 0.9015 0.3847 0.3986 0.3982 0.0136 0.0095 0.2041 0.0653)
  ("eration performs an insertion into the binary search tree and updates the" 0.1366 0.9014 0.4027 0.4162 0.4162 0.0136 0.0095 0.2043 0.0712)
  ("output by splicing the inserted element into the output at the right place. To" 0.1366 0.9016 0.4209 0.4344 0.4344 0.0136 0.0062 0.2818 0.0683)
  ("ﬁnd the right place in the output, we ﬁnd the previous and the next elements" 0.1366 0.9016 0.4389 0.4524 0.4524 0.0136 0.0061 0.2865 0.0386)
  ("in the output by performing a search. These operations can be performed in" 0.1366 0.9018 0.4570 0.4706 0.4706 0.0136 0.0069 0.2769 0.0193)
  ("O(log n) time by careful use of references and data structures." 0.1366 0.7668 0.4752 0.4888 0.4888 0.0136 0.0076 0.2605 0.0568)
  ("The ﬁrst two approaches are not interesting. They improve performance by no" 0.0973 0.9015 0.5058 0.5193 0.5193 0.0136 0.0083 0.2486 0.0399)
  ("more than a logarithmic factor, which would not worth the additional complexity." 0.0973 0.9017 0.5240 0.5375 0.5375 0.0136 0.0050 0.3472 0.0505)
  ("The third approach, however, improves performance by a linear factor, which is" 0.0973 0.9016 0.5420 0.5555 0.5555 0.0136 0.0072 0.2871 0.0399)
  ("very signiﬁcant.^{2}" 0.0973 0.2664 0.5578 0.5737 0.5737 0.0136 0.0073 0.2290 0.0434)
  ("As this example suggests, designing an eﬃcient incremental data structure" 0.1235 0.9016 0.5781 0.5917 0.5917 0.0136 0.0100 0.2536 0.0264)
  ("can be diﬃcult even for a relatively simple problem like sorting. In particular," 0.0973 0.9020 0.5963 0.6099 0.6099 0.0136 0.0088 0.2773 0.0349)
  ("to support eﬃcient incremental updates, we need to use balanced binary search" 0.0973 0.9019 0.6145 0.6280 0.6280 0.0136 0.0074 0.2545 0.0206)
  ("trees, whose design and analysis is signiﬁcantly more complex than that of a" 0.0973 0.9015 0.6325 0.6460 0.6460 0.0136 0.0099 0.2397 0.0546)
  ("sorting algorithm. This suggests that there is a design-complexity gap between" 0.0973 0.9017 0.6507 0.6642 0.6642 0.0136 0.0081 0.2642 0.0699)
  ("conventional static problems (where computation data is not subject to modi-" 0.0973 0.9016 0.6689 0.6824 0.6824 0.0136 0.0089 0.2576 0.1268)
  ("ﬁcations) and their dynamic version that can respond eﬃciently to incremental" 0.0973 0.9017 0.6869 0.7004 0.7004 0.0136 0.0078 0.2601 0.0930)
  ("modiﬁcations. Indeed, the design and analysis of such dynamic data structures" 0.0973 0.9017 0.7050 0.7186 0.7186 0.0136 0.0081 0.2488 0.1412)
  ("has been an active ﬁeld in the algorithms community (Section 10.3). We give" 0.0973 0.9019 0.7232 0.7368 0.7368 0.0136 0.0097 0.2744 0.0337)
  ("more examples of this complexity gap in Section 10.3." 0.0973 0.6442 0.7412 0.7548 0.7548 0.0136 0.0076 0.2988 0.0504)
  ("In addition to the design-complexity gap, there are diﬃculties in using dy-" 0.1235 0.9016 0.7594 0.7730 0.7730 0.0136 0.0097 0.2483 0.0212)
  ("namic data structures for building large software systems, because they are not" 0.0973 0.9017 0.7774 0.7910 0.7910 0.0136 0.0076 0.2568 0.0606)
  ("composable. To see why this is important note that conventional algorithms" 0.0973 0.9018 0.7956 0.8091 0.8091 0.0136 0.0109 0.2504 0.1234)
  ("are directly composable. For example if we have some function f : α → β and" 0.0973 0.9016 0.8099 0.8295 0.8273 0.0136 0.0085 0.2984 0.0312)
  ("g : β → γ, then we can compose these two algorithms, g ◦ f , is well deﬁned" 0.0973 0.9014 0.8279 0.8475 0.8453 0.0136 0.0100 0.2977 0.0111)
  ("and has type α → γ. Concretely, if we want to sort a list of objects and then" 0.0973 0.9013 0.8461 0.8657 0.8635 0.0136 0.0090 0.2648 0.0375)
  ("2" 0.1037 0.1122 0.8749 0.8867 0.8867 0.0118 nil nil 0.0085)
  ("Note that we can support modiﬁcations to the input in (expected) constant time by" 0.1205 0.9017 0.8799 0.8921 0.8921 0.0122 0.0065 0.2712 0.0447)
  ("maintaining a mapping between locations and the pointers to update." 0.1205 0.7757 0.8966 0.9088 0.9088 0.0122 0.0071 0.2629 0.1112))))
